# Project Tooling & Setup

## Package manager — always npm

```bash
npm install <package>       # runtime dependency
npm install -D <package>    # dev dependency
npm uninstall <package>     # uninstall
npm run build               # build
npm ci                      # clean install from the lockfile (what Netlify runs)
```

❌ Never use `pnpm` or `yarn` in this project.
✅ Commit `package-lock.json`. The `.gitignore` ignores `pnpm-lock.yaml` and `yarn.lock`.

### Exactly one committed lockfile

Netlify selects its package manager from whichever lockfile it finds in the
repo. A second lockfile silently changes how the site builds.

This project previously committed `pnpm-lock.yaml` while gitignoring
`package-lock.json`. When `package.json` gained an `overrides` entry pinning
`vite` to `^7.3.2`, the stale pnpm lock did not carry it, so a hosted install
could resolve `vite` 8 again and fail with:

```
[@tailwindcss/vite:generate:build] Missing field `tsconfigPaths` on
BindingViteResolvePluginConfig.resolveOptions
```

`@tailwindcss/vite` installs its own `vite` 8 at the top level, while
`astro` declares `vite ^7.3.2` and the plugin targets vite 7's binding API.
The `overrides` / `pnpm.overrides` pin in `package.json` is what keeps them
on one version — do not remove it, and do not add a second lockfile.

